.Dd November 17, 2026
.Dt TSEARCH 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm tsearch , tfind , tdelete , twalk , tdestroy
.Nd manipulate binary search trees
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In search.h
.Ft void *
.Fn tdelete "const void * restrict key" "posix_tnode ** restrict rootp" "int (*compar) (const void *, const void *)"
.Ft void
.Fn tdestroy "posix_tnode *root" "void (*node_free)(void *)"
.Ft posix_tnode *
.Fn tfind "const void *key" "posix_tnode * const *rootp" "int (*compar) (const void *, const void *)"
.Ft posix_tnode *
.Fn tsearch "const void *key" "posix_tnode **rootp" "int (*compar) (const void *, const void *)"
.Ft void
.Fn twalk "const posix_tnode *root" "void (*action) (const posix_tnode *, VISIT, int)"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn tdelete ,
.Fn tdestroy ,
.Fn tfind ,
.Fn tsearch ,
and
.Fn twalk
functions manage binary search trees.
This implementation uses a balanced AVL tree,
which due to its strong theoretical limit on the height of the tree has
the advantage of calling the comparison function relatively
infrequently.
.Pp
The comparison function passed in by
the user takes two arguments, each of which is a key pointer.
This function has the same style of return values as
.Xr strcmp 3 .
.Pp
The
.Fn tfind
function
searches for a node whose key matches the argument
.Fa key
in the binary tree rooted at
.Fa rootp ,
returning a pointer to the node if it is found and
.Dv NULL
if it is not.
.Pp
The first member of a node is a pointer to its key.
Thus, you should generally cast this result to a
double pointer to the data type stored in the tree, for example
.Vt struct foo ** ,
and use double indirection to retrieve the
original key value.
.Pp
The
.Fn tsearch
function
is identical to
.Fn tfind
except that if no match is found,
it inserts a new node for the
.Fa key
into the tree and returns a pointer to the node.
If
.Fa rootp
points to a
.Dv NULL
value a new binary search tree is created.
.Pp
The
.Fn tdelete
function
deletes a node from the specified binary search tree and returns
a pointer to the parent of the node to be deleted.
It takes the same arguments as
.Fn tfind
and
.Fn tsearch .
If the node to be deleted is the root of the binary search tree,
.Fa rootp
will be adjusted.
.Pp
The
.Fn tdestroy
function destroys the whole search tree, freeing all allocated nodes.
If tree keys need special handling on free, the
.Fa node_free
function can be provided, which is called on each key.
.Pp
The
.Fn twalk
function
walks the binary search tree rooted in
.Fa root
and calls the function
.Fa action
on each node.
The
.Fa action
function
is called with three arguments: a pointer to the current node,
a value from the enum
.Vt VISIT
specifying the traversal type, and a node level
.Pq where level zero is the root of the tree :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
typedef enum { preorder, postorder, endorder, leaf } VISIT;
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Fa action
function is called only once for a leaf node, with the
traversal type
.Dv leaf .
.Pp
Note: the names for the traversal types differ somewhat from
common parlance.
The traversal type
.Dv postorder
corresponds
to what would typically be referred to as in-order, and the
traversal type
.Dv endorder
corresponds to what would typically
be referred to as post-order.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The
.Fn tsearch
function returns
.Dv NULL
if allocation of a new node fails
.Pq usually due to a lack of free memory .
.Pp
The
.Fn tfind ,
.Fn tsearch ,
and
.Fn tdelete
functions
return
.Dv NULL
if
.Fa rootp
is
.Dv NULL
or the node cannot be found.
.Pp
The
.Fn twalk
and
.Fn tdestroy
functions return no value.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example uses
.Fn tsearch
to search for four strings in
.Dv root .
Because the strings are not already present, they are added.
.Fn tsearch
is called twice on the fourth string to demonstrate that
a string is not added when it is already present.
.Fn tfind
is used to find the single instance of the fourth string, and
.Fn tdelete
removes it.
Finally,
.Fn twalk
is used to return and display the resulting binary search tree.
.Bd -literal
#include <stdio.h>
#include <search.h>
#include <string.h>

int
comp(const void *a, const void *b)
{

	return strcmp(a, b);
}

void
printwalk(const posix_tnode * node, VISIT v, int __unused0)
{

	if (v == postorder || v == leaf) {
		printf("node: %s\en", *(char **)node);
	}
}

int
main(void)
{
	posix_tnode *root = NULL;

	char one[] = "blah1";
	char two[] = "blah-2";
	char three[] = "blah-3";
	char four[] = "blah-4";

	tsearch(one, &root, comp);
	tsearch(two, &root, comp);
	tsearch(three, &root, comp);
	tsearch(four, &root, comp);
	tsearch(four, &root, comp);
	printf("four: %s\en", *(char **)tfind(four, &root, comp));
	tdelete(four, &root, comp);

	twalk(root, printwalk);
	tdestroy(root, NULL);
	return 0;
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bsearch 3 ,
.Xr hsearch 3 ,
.Xr lsearch 3
.Sh STANDARDS
These
.Fn tdelete ,
.Fn tfind ,
.Fn tsearch ,
and
.Fn twalk
functions conform to
.St -p1003.1-2008 .
The
.Fn tdestroy
function is a glibc extension.
.Pp
The
.Fa posix_tnode
type is not part of
.St -p1003.1-2008 ,
but is expected to be standardized by future versions of the standard.
It is defined as
.Fa void
for source-level compatibility.
Using
.Fa posix_tnode
makes distinguishing between nodes and keys easier.
